---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Tips">
	<article>
		<header class="space-y-4">
			<h1 class="display-title">Tips</h1>
			<p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-[color:var(--color-muted)] sm:text-xl">
				Practical notes from preparing the boat and the passage.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="page-prose mt-10 max-w-none">
			<h2>Print your own courtesy flags</h2>
			<p>
				You can print your own courtesy flags that match the countries you plan to sail to.
			</p>
			<p>It consists of three simple steps:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Create your own PDF file with the flags you need.</li>
				<li>Send the PDF to a print service that offers fabric printing.</li>
				<li>Cut and sew along the edges of the flags.</li>
			</ol>

			<h3>Make the sheet</h3>
			<p>
				Open
				<a href="https://flag.sailboatadventure.dk">flag.sailboatadventure.dk</a>
				and select the places on your route. The national flag is the courtesy flag. Where an island has its own flag,
				you can add that regional flag beside it. Each finished flag is 45&nbsp;×&nbsp;30&nbsp;cm.
			</p>
			<h3>Have it printed</h3>
			<p>
				Send the PDF to a print service that prints on outdoor fabric. One option is
				<a href="https://www.pixartprinting.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PixartPrinting</a>
				— for example with these settings (same approach as described by
				<a href="https://www.noforeignland.com/help/courtesy-flags" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
					>noforeignland</a
				>):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Material: windproof outdoor fabric, about 115&nbsp;gsm</li>
				<li>Cutting method: square-cut edges</li>
				<li>Reinforced stitching: none</li>
				<li>Reinforced hem: none</li>
				<li>Pole pocket: none</li>
				<li>Accessories: none</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The sheet already includes the fold cloth and the lines you follow when you cut and sew.
			</p>

			<h3>Read the sheet</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>The outer dashed line is where you cut.</li>
				<li>
					The inner dashed line is the fold. There is 2&nbsp;cm of cloth outside the finished flag on every side. The
					picture is mirrored across that fold, so the colour continues into the hem.
				</li>
				<li>
					The country name is printed in the left fold. That edge is the hoist, the side you attach to the halyard, and
					the name shows which way is up.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Sew the flag</h3>
			<ol>
				<li>Cut along the outer dashed line.</li>
				<li>
					Fold the top and the bottom in along the inner dashed line, and stitch twice along each edge.
				</li>
				<li>
					Fold the fly in along the inner dashed line — the fly is the free end, away from the halyard — and stitch a
					triple edge.
				</li>
				<li>
					On the hoist, fold a short piece of webbing over about 50&nbsp;cm of 3&nbsp;mm braided polyester line, and
					stitch the webbing to the flag so the line is held inside it. A small loop at the top makes it easy to see
					which way is up when you change flags on deck.
				</li>
				<li>Write the country name on the webbing with a permanent pen, so you can grab the right flag in a hurry.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
